<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Cognition is more about the whole structure and action of thought (e.g., conscious or unconscious decision-making, explicit reasoning, learning, memory, etc.), rather than just indicating conscious thought. So cognitive as an adjective is just kind of&#8230; broad, only obliquely related to your context, and not giving the exact relationship you intend&#8230; I guess.</p>
<p>I'm not aware of a specific term for dream-space in experimental psychology (the interpretation of dreams being a Freudian thing, which is most often to say arbitrary and not evidence-driven), but I know the term unconscious is still in quite common use in various meanings. Full disclosure, though, sleep research is well outside of my specialty (but I am very much in cognitive psych.)</p>
<p>All in all, it wouldn't sound as cool, but nonconscious would be much closer to your meaning.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Still, the made-up term is nifty, so again, not complaining.
<p>I think if I have any complaint about the piece, it's that the physical manifestation portion of the fellow's anomaly is boring and feels pretty tacked on to the much more interesting dream narrative. I think the description was also somewhat difficult to understand, but that's partially because it's such a vague, mushy power. Trying to piece together the whole Philadelphia Experiment bit took some effort.</p>
<p>In any case, very entertaining and well done overall. I'm extremely enjoying your group's content for this contest (and I love the tale tie-in), and look forward to more. +1</p>

							</div>

								
